# Line endings — settled 2026-09-04.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows and deployed to a Linux host, and every text file
# is already stored LF in the index. With no rule here, a Windows checkout under
# core.autocrlf=true wants CRLF in the working tree, so git prints "LF will be replaced by
# CRLF" on almost every `git add`. Nothing was ever broken by that warning — but the same
# ambiguity did hide a real defect once: a driver message that embedded a filesystem path
# read differently on the two platforms, and the difference was only caught by comparing a
# generated file across them (see WORKLOG 2026-09-03).
#
# ⚠️ Rule order matters: LATER lines win.

# 1. Default — text is normalised to LF in the index and checked out as LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# 2. Windows-hosted formats keep CRLF in the working tree. The .bas modules are imported by
#    Excel's VBA editor and the scripts are run by cmd.exe and PowerShell; CRLF is what those
#    tools expect, and it is what the four files driving the verified Excel gate
#    (RysePricingBridge.bas, JsonConverter.bas, TestHarness.bas, Run-BridgeTests.ps1)
#    already carry. Without these lines rule 1 would flip them to LF on the next checkout.
*.bas text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# 3. Data files are byte-frozen: no EOL conversion in either direction. The curve exports and
#    CSVs come from 47 as LF while the older *_Yield_Curve.txt exports carry CRLF, and local
#    autocrlf must rewrite neither — see CLAUDE.md, the "CRLF blocks pull" trap of 2026-07-18.
data/** -text
